  <title>Import DTN2hops</title>
  <p>DTN2hops is a lab of four virtual machines, <sys>node1</sys> to
  <sys>node4</sys>, five switches, a switch wrapper and three channel
  emulators. It comes in two files, which you have downloaded into your
  <file>Downloads</file> folder:</p>
  <list>
    <item><p><file>DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026.vbp</file>, the archive of the lab:
    its bricks, and the private copy of the disk of each machine, with what
    was done in it;</p></item>
    <item><p><file>debian13.tar.gz</file>, which holds the disk image that
    the private copies are made on, <file>debian13.qcow2</file>. The archive
    doesn't hold it.</p></item>
  </list>
  <p>Before you start, extract <file>debian13.qcow2</file> into
  <file>Downloads</file>, where it takes 6.8 GB. In a terminal:</p>
  <screen>cd ~/Downloads
tar -xzf debian13.tar.gz</screen>
  <p>You import the lab into Virtualbricks, started on your computer, the one
  the two files are on. The lab was made with Virtualbricks 2.1, and
  Virtualbricks converts it while it reads the archive. An archive of this
  version imports the same way.</p>
  <section id="open">
    <title>Open the archive</title>
    <steps>
      <item>
        <p>Choose <guiseq><gui>Projects</gui><gui>Import…</gui></guiseq>. The
        Import Project window opens, and asks for the archive.</p>
        <figure>
          <title>The menu Projects</title>
          <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/import-menu.png">
            <p>The main window, with the menu Projects open: Projects…, New
            Project…, Open Recent, Save, Duplicate…, Rename…, Import… and
            Export….</p>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>Click the button of the file, which says <gui>(None)</gui>. In the
        window that opens, click <gui>Home</gui>, double-click
        <file>Downloads</file>, choose
        <file>DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026.vbp</file>, and click <gui>Open</gui>. The
        Import Project window reads the archive, then shows what it
        holds.</p>
        <figure>
          <title>The archive, in Downloads</title>
          <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/import-archive.png">
            <p>The window Choose an archive, in the folder Downloads of the
            home, with DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026.vbp chosen, and the button Open
            at the bottom right.</p>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </item>
    </steps>
  </section>
  <section id="options">
    <title>What the window offers</title>
    <figure>
      <title>The Import Project window, with the lab</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/import.png">
        <p>The Import Project window for
        ~/Downloads/DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026.vbp: the name DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026;
        3 netemu, 4 qemu, 5 switch and 1 switchwrapper; the image debian13,
        used by node1.hda to node4.hda, with Copy grey and Leave unset
        chosen; Open the project after the import, on.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Name</gui></title>
        <p><sys>DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026</sys>, the name of the file. If a
        project has it already, the window suggests
        <sys>DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026-2</sys>: an import never replaces a
        project. You can change it; under it, the window says why a name
        can't be a project's: it's empty, has a <sys>/</sys>, starts with a
        dot, is too long, or is a project's already.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>The bricks</title>
        <p>Below the name, how many bricks of each kind the project has, by
        the names of the project file: <sys>netemu</sys> are the channel
        emulators, <sys>qemu</sys> the virtual machines.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Images</gui></title>
        <p>A row for <sys>debian13</sys>, used by the disk
        <sys>hda</sys> of each of the four machines, with three
        choices:</p>
        <list>
          <item><p><gui>Copy</gui>, from the archive to the shared images of
          your workspace. It's grey: the archive doesn't hold the image, and
          there is nothing to copy; its tooltip says <gui>Not in the
          archive</gui>. In a large archive, the window may look for the
          image for a while: <gui>Copy</gui> is chosen meanwhile, the row
          says <gui>Looking in the archive…</gui>, and <gui>Copy</gui> turns
          grey once the window has read the archive to its end.</p></item>
          <item><p><gui>Use a file…</gui> of this computer, which the project
          uses where it is.</p></item>
          <item><p><gui>Leave unset</gui>, for its disks to get an image
          later.</p></item>
        </list>
        <p><gui>Leave unset</gui> is chosen: the window looked for the image
        where the project had it,
        <file>/home/carlo/VB_images/debian13.qcow2</file> on the computer the
        lab was made on, and in your shared images, as
        <file>debian13.qcow2</file> or <file>debian13</file>, and found it in
        neither. It doesn't look in <file>Downloads</file>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>This computer's paths</gui></title>
        <p>Not there at all for this lab. It shows when the project names the
        folders of QEMU and VDE of the computer it comes from, and they
        aren't yours; a project of Virtualbricks 2.1 doesn't name them.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Open the project after the import</gui></title>
        <p>On: once imported, the lab opens in the main window.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Import</gui></title>
        <p>Grey until the window has read the archive, and whenever something
        stops the import; the window says what. Under the name: a name that
        can't be a project's. At the bottom, in red: a file chosen with
        <gui>Use a file…</gui> that isn't there any more, or a name too long
        for the names of the bricks. Their sockets are in a folder named
        after the project, and the path of a socket has at most 107 bytes;
        the longest name of this lab, <sys>sat_channel_emulator</sys>, has
        20.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="image">
    <title>Choose the image</title>
    <note style="warning">
      <p>For this tutorial, choose <gui>Use a file…</gui>, then
      <file>debian13.qcow2</file>: not <gui>Copy</gui>, nor <gui>Leave
      unset</gui>.</p>
    </note>
    <steps>
      <item>
        <p>In the row of <sys>debian13</sys>, click <gui>Use a file…</gui>. In
        the window that opens, click <gui>Home</gui>, double-click
        <file>Downloads</file>, choose <file>debian13.qcow2</file>, the file
        you extracted from <file>debian13.tar.gz</file>, and click
        <gui>Open</gui>.</p>
        <figure>
          <title>The image, in Downloads</title>
          <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/import-image.png">
            <p>The window The file of debian13, in the folder Downloads of the
            home, with DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026.vbp and debian13.qcow2, which is
            chosen, and the button Open at the bottom right.</p>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </item>
    </steps>
    <p>The row now says <gui>Uses ~/Downloads/debian13.qcow2</gui>. The import
    points the private copies of the four machines at it, so that they keep
    what was done in them. It must be the image the lab was made on: a
    private copy holds only the changes over its image, and over another one
    the machine finds a damaged disk.</p>
    <p>The other two choices don't do for this lab:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Copy</gui></title>
        <p>Grey: the archive doesn't hold the image.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Leave unset</gui></title>
        <p>The project would come without the image <sys>debian13</sys>, and
        the disks of the four machines without an image. Their private
        copies would stay made on the file of the computer the lab comes
        from. When you gave the disks an image, in their settings, the first
        start of each machine would move its private copy aside, as
        <file>node1_hda.cow.bak-</file> and the date, and make a new one,
        empty: the machines would start from the image as it is, without
        what was done in them.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <note style="tip">
      <p>The project uses <file>debian13.qcow2</file> where it is: keep it in
      <file>Downloads</file>. To keep it with your other images instead, move
      it into the <link xref="images#shared">shared images</link> of your
      workspace before the import: there, the window finds it, and chooses
      <gui>Use a file…</gui> with it by itself.</p>
    </note>
  </section>
  <section id="import">
    <title>Import</title>
    <steps>
      <item>
        <p>Click <gui>Import</gui>. The window shows the progress, and
        <gui>Cancel</gui> becomes <gui>Stop</gui>: an import stopped removes
        what it wrote. You can close the window, and let the import
        finish.</p>
        <figure>
          <title>Ready to import</title>
          <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/import-ready.png">
            <p>The Import Project window, with Use a file… chosen for
            debian13, which uses ~/Downloads/debian13.qcow2, and the button
            Import at the top right.</p>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </item>
    </steps>
    <p>Once imported, the lab opens: the main window shows
    <sys>DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026</sys>, with its thirteen bricks, all
    stopped.</p>
    <figure>
      <title>The lab, imported</title>
      <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/import-done.png">
        <p>The main window of the project DTN2hops_26_Feb_2026, on its tab
        Bricks: 0 of 13 running; sw1 to sw4, switchwrapper, sw5,
        channel_emulator, sat_channel_emulator, to_host_ch_emulator, then
        node1, all stopped, with the start button of switchwrapper
        grey.</p>
      </media>
    </figure>
    <p>If a brick of the project open before runs, the lab is imported but
    doesn't open: stop the bricks, then open it from the Projects window.</p>
    <p>The start button of <sys>switchwrapper</sys> is grey: a switch wrapper
    is a switch that another program runs, at
    <file>/var/run/switch/sck</file>, and it runs while that program's switch
    is there. See <link xref="kinds"/>.</p>
  </section>
</page>
